   There's a comment in "pickup.c" indicating that the DevTeam is (or
was at one time) aware of the issue, although it's not actually listed
as a "bug":

     /* this isn't really right, since any form of observation
        (telepathic or monster/object/food detection) ought to
        force the determination of alive vs dead state; but basing
        it just on opening the box is much simpler to cope with */

Notice that any patch purporting to deal with the "observe the box"
problem should deal not only with wands of probing, but also with
Detect Monsters, Detect Objects (i.e., the cat corpse), ESP, and maybe
even warning, if a housecat is high-level enough to trigger warning
for a level-1 character (which I don't think it is, but you'd have to
check).
   But then at that point, what would be the difference between 
Schroedinger's "superposition of states" and just plain putting a
live or dead cat in the box at the moment it's created? The player
would see the same thing in both cases, and the joke would be lost.

